E+=[
X('narmer-palette','Narmer Palette',H,'confirmed',2,-3100,['archaeological','iconographic'],
 "The Narmer Palette is a ceremonial siltstone palette about 64 cm tall, found in 1897–98 by James Quibell and Frederick Green in the temple deposit at Hierakonpolis, Upper Egypt, and now in the Egyptian Museum, Cairo. It shows King Narmer wearing the White Crown of Upper Egypt on one face and the Red Crown of Lower Egypt on the other, smiting an enemy and inspecting rows of decapitated captives, with intertwined long-necked 'serpopards' at the centre. It dates to about 3100 BCE and is often presented as one of the earliest images of the unification of Egypt.",
 [('Record of a real conquest','Many Egyptologists read the scenes as commemorating an actual military unification of north and south under Narmer.'),
  ('Ritual and symbolic image','Others argue the palette shows idealised royal ideology (the king as bringer of order over chaos) rather than a specific historical battle, and that unification was a longer process.'),
  ('Narmer, Menes or Aha','Classical and later king lists name Menes as Egypt\'s first king; whether Menes was Narmer, his successor Hor-Aha, or a composite figure is still debated.')],
 ['wiki:Narmer_Palette','wiki:Narmer'],img='Narmer_Palette',dateNote='c. 3100 BCE (late Predynastic / Dynasty 0)'),
X('diary-of-merer','Diary of Merer (Wadi al-Jarf papyri)',H,'confirmed',2,-2560,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In 2013 a French–Egyptian mission led by Pierre Tallet found papyrus fragments at the ancient harbour of Wadi al-Jarf on the Red Sea coast. They date to about year 27 of King Khufu's reign and are the oldest inscribed papyri yet known. The best preserved, the logbook of an official named Merer, records his team of about 40 men moving limestone blocks by boat from the Tura quarries along the Nile and canals to Giza, under the oversight of Ankhhaf, Khufu's half-brother.",
 [('Eyewitness to pyramid building','Most Egyptologists see the diary as direct documentary evidence that Khufu\'s workforce supplied the Great Pyramid, consistent with the dynastic Egyptian construction of the monument.'),
  ('Only casing stone','Some alternative writers argue the papyri cover only the transport of fine Tura limestone casing blocks late in the project, and so say nothing on who built or planned the core; mainstream scholars reply that the diary fits a wider body of evidence (workers\' town, quarries, tombs of overseers).'),
  ('Older-structure theories','Proponents of a much older or lost-civilisation origin for the Giza pyramids regard the diary as evidence of Egyptian work on, or renovation of, the site rather than original construction; this view is speculative and not supported by excavators.')],
 ['wiki:Diary_of_Merer','wiki:Wadi_al-Jarf'],img=None,dateNote='c. 2560 BCE (year 26–27 of Khufu)'),
X('amarna-letters','Amarna letters',H,'confirmed',2,-1360,['textual','archaeological'],
 "The Amarna letters are an archive of about 380 clay tablets, mostly in Akkadian cuneiform, discovered in 1887 at Tell el-Amarna, the capital founded by Akhenaten. They contain diplomatic correspondence between the Egyptian court (Amenhotep III, Akhenaten and perhaps their successors) and the great kings of Babylonia, Assyria, Mitanni, Hatti and Alashiya (Cyprus), as well as many letters from vassal rulers in Canaan and Syria, dating to about 1360–1332 BCE. The tablets are now spread across museums including the British Museum, Berlin and Cairo.",
 [('A Late Bronze Age "club of great powers"','Historians use the letters to reconstruct an international system of royal \'brothers\' exchanging gold, brides and gifts before the Late Bronze Age collapse.'),
  ('Habiru and the Hebrews','Several letters complain about the Habiru (ʿApiru), unsettled groups raiding Canaanite cities. Since the 19th century some have linked the term to the biblical Hebrews; many scholars now treat Habiru as a social label for outsiders rather than an ethnic name, though the debate continues.'),
  ('Biblical-era identifications','Some writers have attempted to match Amarna rulers such as Abdi-Heba of Jerusalem or Labaya of Shechem with biblical figures; these identifications are speculative and depend on contested chronologies.')],
 ['wiki:Amarna_letters','wiki:Habiru'],img='Amarna_letters',end=-1332,dateNote='c. 1360–1332 BCE'),
X('battle-of-kadesh','Battle of Kadesh and the first peace treaty',H,'confirmed',2,-1274,['textual','archaeological','iconographic'],
 "In about 1274 BCE Ramesses II of Egypt and Muwatalli II of the Hittite Empire fought near Kadesh on the Orontes River in Syria, in what is often called the largest chariot battle known. Ramesses had his near-defeat and personal heroism carved on temple walls at Abu Simbel, Karnak, Luxor and the Ramesseum (the 'Poem' and 'Bulletin'). Kadesh stayed in Hittite hands, and about 16 years later the two powers concluded a treaty, preserved in both an Egyptian hieroglyphic version and an Akkadian cuneiform version found at Hattusa, often described as the earliest surviving peace treaty.",
 [('Egyptian victory','Ramesses II\'s inscriptions present the battle as a great triumph won almost single-handedly by the king with divine help.'),
  ('Hittite strategic success','Many historians conclude that the Hittites achieved their aims, since Egypt withdrew and Hittite control of Kadesh and Amurru continued; Hittite texts allude to Egyptian defeat.'),
  ('Draw and propaganda','A common middle view is that the battle was tactically indecisive and that the monumental Egyptian accounts are a classic example of royal propaganda shaping the record.')],
 ['wiki:Battle_of_Kadesh','wiki:Egyptian–Hittite_peace_treaty'],img='Battle_of_Kadesh',dateNote='battle c. 1274 BCE; treaty c. 1259 BCE'),
X('osiris-myth','Osiris, Isis, Set and Horus',M,'myth-legend',1,-2400,['textual','mythological'],
 "The Osiris myth is the best-known story of ancient Egyptian religion. The god-king Osiris is murdered by his brother Set; his sister-wife Isis searches for and restores his body, conceives their son Horus, and Osiris becomes ruler of the dead, while Horus contends with Set for the throne. Allusions appear in the Pyramid Texts of the 24th century BCE, but the fullest continuous narrative is the Greek writer Plutarch's 'On Isis and Osiris' (c. 100 CE), which gives the familiar detail of the body cut into fourteen pieces. The myth underpinned Egyptian kingship (the living king as Horus, the dead king as Osiris) and funerary beliefs for millennia.",
 [('Myth of kingship and agriculture','Egyptologists read it as a charter for royal succession and as linked to the annual Nile flood and the renewal of grain.'),
  ('Dying-and-rising god','J. G. Frazer grouped Osiris with Adonis, Attis and Tammuz as a \'dying-and-rising god\'; later scholars such as Jonathan Z. Smith criticised the category as too broad, and the debate continues.'),
  ('Sky readings','Ancient texts identify Osiris with the constellation Orion and Isis with Sirius; some alternative writers build on this (for example the Orion correlation idea for Giza), which most Egyptologists regard as speculative.'),
  ('A deified ancient ruler','Euhemerist readings, from antiquity to some modern alternative authors, suggest Osiris was a real early civiliser king; there is no archaeological evidence for this.')],
 ['wiki:Osiris_myth','wiki:Osiris'],img='Osiris_myth',dateNote='earliest allusions Pyramid Texts c. 24th century BCE; Plutarch c. 100 CE'),
X('abu-simbel','Abu Simbel: the sun temple that was moved',H,'confirmed',2,-1264,['archaeological','astronomical'],
 "The two rock-cut temples at Abu Simbel in Nubia were carved for Ramesses II and his queen Nefertari in the 13th century BCE (work is usually dated c. 1264–1244 BCE). Covered by sand, the Great Temple was seen by Johann Ludwig Burckhardt in 1813 and entered by Giovanni Belzoni in 1817. Threatened by Lake Nasser behind the Aswan High Dam, both temples were cut into large blocks and reassembled about 65 m higher and some 200 m back between 1964 and 1968, in a UNESCO-led international campaign. Twice a year, in late February and late October, the rising sun reaches the inner sanctuary and lights statues of Ra-Horakhty, Amun and the deified Ramesses, leaving the underworld-linked Ptah largely in shadow.",
 [('Deliberate solar alignment','Most researchers accept that the axis was oriented so sunlight reaches the sanctuary on particular dates, part of the temple\'s solar theology.'),
  ('Birthday and coronation dates','A widely repeated claim says the two dates mark Ramesses II\'s birthday and coronation; this is a modern popular tradition without known ancient documentation.'),
  ('Did the move shift the effect?','Reports state that after the relocation the illumination occurs about a day later than before; how precisely the original builders fixed the dates is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Abu_Simbel',f'url:{U}88/|UNESCO: Nubian Monuments from Abu Simbel to Philae|heritage'],img='Abu_Simbel',end=-1244),
X('tel-dan-stele','Tel Dan stele ("House of David")',H,'disputed',2,-840,['archaeological','epigraphic'],
 "Fragments of a basalt stele inscribed in Old Aramaic were found in 1993 and 1994 at Tel Dan in northern Israel by Avraham Biran's excavation team. The text, usually dated to the 9th century BCE and attributed to a king of Aram-Damascus (often identified as Hazael), boasts of killing the king of Israel and a king of the 'bytdwd'. Most scholars read this as 'House of David', which would make it the earliest widely accepted reference to David's dynasty outside the Bible. It is displayed at the Israel Museum.",
 [('House of David','The majority view takes bytdwd as the name of the Judahite royal dynasty, supporting the existence of a historical David as dynastic founder, though not the biblical details of his reign.'),
  ('Alternative readings','A minority of scholars proposed other readings, such as a place name, a temple (\'house of the beloved\') or a divine epithet.'),
  ('Authenticity questions','Shortly after discovery some critics suggested the fragments might be forged or planted; this was not widely accepted, as they were found in excavation contexts.')],
 ['wiki:Tel_Dan_stele','wiki:Tel_Dan'],img='Tel_Dan_stele',dateNote='9th century BCE (c. 870–750 range proposed)'),
X('mesha-stele','Mesha Stele (Moabite Stone)',H,'confirmed',2,-840,['archaeological','epigraphic','textual'],
 "The Mesha Stele is a basalt inscription of about 34 lines set up around 840 BCE by Mesha, king of Moab, at Dhiban in present-day Jordan. Seen by the missionary F. A. Klein in 1868, it was broken by local people before it could be removed; the text was recovered thanks to a paper squeeze made for Charles Clermont-Ganneau, and the reassembled stone is in the Louvre. Mesha describes Moab's oppression by Omri, king of Israel, and his son, his successful revolt, and his dedication of Israelite spoils to the god Chemosh; it contains one of the earliest known extra-biblical references to Israel and to the god YHWH.",
 [('Same war, other side','Historians compare it with 2 Kings 3, which tells of Mesha\'s rebellion from the Israelite viewpoint; the accounts differ on outcome and details, illustrating how each side recorded events.'),
  ('A second "House of David"?','In 1994 André Lemaire proposed that a damaged line reads \'House of David\'; others have suggested different readings (including a name such as Balak), and the passage remains debated.'),
  ('Divine wrath theology','The inscription explains defeat as the anger of Chemosh, closely paralleling biblical explanations of Israel\'s misfortunes, which scholars see as shared regional religious thinking.')],
 ['wiki:Mesha_Stele','wiki:Moab'],img='Mesha_Stele',dateNote='c. 840 BCE'),
X('natufian-culture','Natufians: the last hunter-gatherers before farming',H,'confirmed',2,-13000,['archaeological','radiocarbon'],
 "The Natufian culture flourished in the Levant from roughly 15,000 to 11,500 years ago. Natufians lived in semi-permanent villages of round stone houses, harvested wild cereals with sickles, buried their dead beneath their homes (sometimes with dogs or puppies, as at Ain Mallaha) and produced early decorated art. Residues from Raqefet Cave in Israel have been interpreted as evidence of beer-like brewing about 13,000 years ago. Many archaeologists see the Natufians as the bridge between foraging and the first farming communities of the Neolithic.",
 [('Climate push','A long-standing view links the Younger Dryas cold spell (c. 12,900–11,700 years ago) to stresses that encouraged Natufians to cultivate cereals; others argue the transition was gradual and driven by social factors.'),
  ('Feasting and beer first','Some researchers suggest feasting and brewing, rather than bread, helped motivate early cereal cultivation; this is debated.'),
  ('Abu Hureyra and a cosmic impact','Proponents of the Younger Dryas impact hypothesis argue that melted glass and other markers at Abu Hureyra in Syria record an airburst affecting a late Natufian–early farming village (Moore et al. 2020); critics dispute both the evidence and its interpretation.')],
 ['wiki:Natufian_culture','wiki:Abu_Hureyra','doi:10.1016/j.jasrep.2018.08.008'],img='Natufian_culture',end=-9500,dateNote='c. 15,000–11,500 years ago'),
X('proto-sinaitic-script','Proto-Sinaitic: the first alphabet',H,'confirmed',2,-1850,['epigraphic','archaeological'],
 "Short inscriptions found by Flinders Petrie in 1904–05 at the turquoise mines of Serabit el-Khadim in Sinai use signs adapted from Egyptian hieroglyphs to write a Semitic language, each sign standing for the first sound of the word it pictures (an ox head, ʾalp, for ʾ). In 1916 Alan Gardiner read the recurring sequence l-bʿlt, 'to Baʿalat'. Similar inscriptions found at Wadi el-Hol in Egypt in the 1990s by John and Deborah Darnell are dated to around 1900–1800 BCE. This script is regarded as the ancestor of Phoenician and through it of Hebrew, Aramaic, Arabic, Greek and Latin letters.",
 [('Invented by workers','Orly Goldwasser has argued that the alphabet was invented by non-literate Canaanite miners or soldiers who borrowed hieroglyphic pictures without learning Egyptian scribal rules; others favour bilingual scribes or officials as the inventors.'),
  ('Dating disputes','Dates for the Sinai inscriptions range from the 19th to the 15th century BCE depending on archaeological and palaeographic arguments.'),
  ('Hebrew and Exodus readings','Some writers, such as Douglas Petrovich, have claimed the inscriptions are early Hebrew and connected to the Israelites in Egypt; most epigraphers reject these readings as unsupported.')],
 ['wiki:Proto-Sinaitic_script','wiki:Serabit_el-Khadim','wiki:Wadi_el-Hol'],img='Proto-Sinaitic_script',dateNote='c. 1900–1500 BCE (debated)'),
]
# --- part 2: Ottoman / Safavid, Caribbean, Central America
E+=[
X('battle-of-vienna-1683','Siege of Vienna 1683 (and the croissant legend)',H,'confirmed',2,1683,['textual','archaeological'],
 "In July 1683 an Ottoman army under Grand Vizier Kara Mustafa Pasha besieged Vienna, the Habsburg capital. On 12 September a relief army of Polish, Imperial and German forces, led by King Jan III Sobieski of Poland, attacked from the Kahlenberg heights; a massed cavalry charge including the Polish winged hussars broke the besiegers. Kara Mustafa was executed in Belgrade that December on the Sultan's orders, and the defeat opened the long Habsburg counter-offensive in Hungary. The siege also gathered famous legends about the origins of Viennese food and coffee.",
 [('Turning point','Many historians treat 1683 as the moment Ottoman expansion into central Europe ended; others stress that Ottoman decline was gradual and that the empire remained a major power for long after.'),
  ('Croissant legend','A popular story says bakers invented the crescent-shaped croissant to celebrate the victory; food historians note that crescent-shaped Kipferl breads are older and that the French croissant is a 19th-century development, so the tale is treated as legend.'),
  ('Coffee legend','Another tradition credits the spy Jerzy Franciszek Kulczycki with opening Vienna\'s first coffee house using sacks of coffee left by the Ottomans; records show an Armenian, Johannes Diodato, received an early coffee licence in 1685, and the Kulczycki story is regarded as embellished.')],
 ['wiki:Battle_of_Vienna','wiki:Jerzy_Franciszek_Kulczycki'],img='Battle_of_Vienna'),
X('devshirme-janissaries','Devshirme and the Janissaries',H,'confirmed',2,1380,['textual'],
 "The Janissaries were the elite standing infantry of the Ottoman Empire, formed in the 14th century. From about the late 14th century they were recruited largely through the devshirme, a periodic levy of boys, mainly from Christian families in the Balkans, who were converted to Islam, trained and enrolled as the Sultan's servants; the most able rose to the highest offices of state, including grand vizier. The levy faded in the 17th century, and after the Janissaries became a powerful and rebellious political force, Sultan Mahmud II destroyed the corps in the 'Auspicious Incident' of 1826.",
 [('Enslavement and forced conversion','Many historians, and Balkan national traditions, describe the devshirme as a form of slavery that took children from their families and faiths.'),
  ('Route to power','Other historians emphasise that devshirme recruits could reach great wealth and power, and note accounts of some families seeking to have sons taken; both aspects are documented.'),
  ('Secret brotherhood','The Janissaries\' close ties to the Bektashi Sufi order gave rise to stories of secret rites and heterodox beliefs; the extent of Bektashi influence on the corps is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Devshirme','wiki:Janissary','wiki:Auspicious_Incident'],img='Devshirme',end=1826,dateNote='Janissaries c. 1363–1826; devshirme c. late 14th–17th centuries'),
X('naqsh-e-jahan','Naqsh-e Jahan Square, Isfahan',H,'confirmed',3,1598,['archaeological','textual'],
 "After Shah Abbas I made Isfahan the Safavid capital in 1598, he laid out the vast Naqsh-e Jahan ('Image of the World') square, about 560 m long. It is framed by the Shah (Imam) Mosque, the Sheikh Lotfollah Mosque, the Ali Qapu palace and the gateway to the Qeysarieh bazaar, bringing royal, religious and commercial power together in one planned space. It was inscribed on UNESCO's World Heritage List in 1979 and is often cited as a high point of Safavid architecture.",
 [('Statement of state and faith','Historians read the square as a deliberate display of Safavid kingship and of Twelver Shiism as the state religion.'),
  ('Commerce first','Others stress the economic role of the square and bazaar in Abbas\'s policy of drawing the silk trade and foreign merchants to Isfahan.'),
  ('Acoustic marvels','Guides often describe multiple echoes under the Shah Mosque dome and the Ali Qapu music room\'s sound-absorbing niches; such acoustic effects are real but popular claims about their exact design intent are not well documented.')],
 ['wiki:Naqsh-e_Jahan_Square',f'url:{U}115/|UNESCO: Meidan Emam, Esfahan|heritage'],img='Naqsh-e_Jahan_Square',dateNote='laid out from 1598; main buildings c. 1602–1630s'),
X('shah-ismail','Shah Ismail I: the boy king worshipped as divine',H,'confirmed',2,1501,['textual'],
 "Ismail I (1487–1524), head of the Safavid Sufi order, was about 14 when his Qizilbash followers captured Tabriz in 1501 and proclaimed him Shah. He made Twelver Shi'ism the state religion of Iran, shaping the country's religious identity to the present. His Turkic-speaking Qizilbash followers venerated him as a divine or messianic figure, and in poems written under the pen name Khata'i he described himself in exalted, even divine terms. His defeat by the Ottoman Sultan Selim I at Chaldiran in 1514, where Ottoman artillery and firearms proved decisive, damaged the belief in his invincibility.",
 [('Messianic leader','Historians see Ismail\'s rise as an example of a millenarian movement: devotees believed he was an emanation of God or the forerunner of the Hidden Imam.'),
  ('Political strategy','Other scholars emphasise his use of religious charisma and enforced conversion to build a state distinct from the Sunni Ottomans.'),
  ('After Chaldiran','Contemporary sources say Ismail withdrew from active campaigning after 1514; how much his followers\' faith in him declined is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Ismail_I','wiki:Battle_of_Chaldiran','wiki:Qizilbash'],img='Ismail_I',end=1524,dateNote='proclaimed Shah 1501; died 1524'),
X('haitian-revolution','Haitian Revolution',H,'confirmed',1,1791,['textual'],
 "Beginning with an uprising of enslaved people in the northern plain of Saint-Domingue in August 1791, the Haitian Revolution became the only slave revolt to found an independent state. Leaders including Toussaint Louverture and Jean-Jacques Dessalines fought French, Spanish and British forces, and defeated Napoleon's expedition of 1802–03. Dessalines declared the independence of Haiti on 1 January 1804. In 1825 France demanded an indemnity of 150 million francs (later reduced to 90 million) in return for recognition, a debt Haiti paid off over many decades.",
 [('Silenced revolution','Historian Michel-Rolph Trouillot argued that the revolution was long \'silenced\' in Western histories because it was unthinkable to contemporaries; it is now studied as central to the Age of Revolutions.'),
  ('Role of Vodou','The Bois Caïman ceremony is remembered as the spiritual start of the uprising; historians debate how much is documented and how much is later tradition.'),
  ('A "pact with the devil"','Some modern religious commentators have claimed Haiti\'s founders made a pact with the devil at Bois Caïman; historians and Haitian scholars regard this as a polemical reinterpretation without historical basis.')],
 ['wiki:Haitian_Revolution','wiki:Haiti_indemnity_controversy'],img='Haitian_Revolution',end=1804),
X('kalinago-caribs','Kalinago ("Island Caribs") and the cannibal label',H,'disputed',2,1493,['textual','oral tradition','archaeological'],
 "The Kalinago are the Indigenous people of the Lesser Antilles whom Europeans called Caribs. Spanish accounts from Columbus's second voyage (1493) onwards described them as man-eaters; the word 'cannibal' itself derives from the Spanish name for the Caribs. In 1503 the Spanish Crown allowed people classified as cannibals to be enslaved, which gave colonists an incentive to apply the label. Kalinago resisted European settlement for centuries, and today a Kalinago Territory exists on Dominica.",
 [('Ritual cannibalism happened','Some historians and archaeologists accept that limited ritual consumption of enemies occurred, based on early accounts and finds of human remains.'),
  ('Colonial slander','Others argue that cannibalism accusations were largely a colonial invention or exaggeration used to justify conquest and slavery, pointing to the lack of reliable eyewitness reports.'),
  ('Two languages, one people','Chroniclers recorded that Kalinago men and women spoke differently and explained this by a legend that mainland Carib warriors had conquered Arawak-speaking islanders and taken their women; linguists note the language was Arawakan with a Carib-derived men\'s jargon, and the conquest story is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Kalinago','wiki:Island_Caribs'],img='Kalinago',dateNote='first European accounts 1493; Kalinago Territory established 1903'),
X('joya-de-ceren','Joya de Cerén: the Maya village under the ash',H,'confirmed',2,600,['archaeological','volcanological'],
 "Joya de Cerén in El Salvador is a farming village buried under several metres of ash by the eruption of the nearby Loma Caldera vent around 600 CE. Found in 1976 when a bulldozer cut into a building, and excavated by Payson Sheets and others, it preserves houses, storehouses, a sweat bath, a communal building, gardens and manioc fields, with pots, food remains and tools left where they were used. It was listed by UNESCO in 1993 and is often called the 'Pompeii of the Americas'.",
 [('Everyday Maya life','Archaeologists value it as a rare snapshot of ordinary commoner households rather than elite temples and kings.'),
  ('Where did the people go?','No human remains have been found, suggesting the villagers had warning and fled; whether they survived the ash fall is unknown.'),
  ('Shaman\'s house','One structure has been interpreted as a building used by a diviner or shaman, based on its contents; this identification is an interpretation.')],
 ['wiki:Joya_de_Cerén',f'url:{U}675/|UNESCO: Joya de Cerén Archaeological Site|heritage'],img='Joya_de_Cerén',dateNote='buried c. 600 CE (dates c. 590–660 proposed)'),
X('copan-stairway','Copán and the Hieroglyphic Stairway',H,'confirmed',2,755,['archaeological','epigraphic'],
 "Copán, in western Honduras, was a Classic Maya kingdom ruled by a dynasty founded in 426 CE by K'inich Yax K'uk' Mo'. Its Hieroglyphic Stairway, dedicated in 755 CE under the ruler K'ak' Yipyaj Chan K'awiil, carries about 2,200 glyphs, the longest known Maya inscription, recounting the dynasty's history. The stairway collapsed and when it was restored in the 1930s many blocks were reset out of order, so the text is only partly readable in sequence. Altar Q portrays all sixteen kings of the dynasty. Copán was listed by UNESCO in 1980.",
 [('A foreign founder','Isotope analysis of a skeleton thought to be Yax K\'uk\' Mo\' suggests he grew up outside Copán, and his imagery shows links to Teotihuacan; scholars debate whether he was an outsider installed by central Mexican power or a local claimant using prestige symbols.'),
  ('Scrambled history','Epigraphers have reconstructed parts of the stairway\'s order using old photographs and drawings, but large portions remain uncertain.'),
  ('Collapse of the kingdom','Copán declined in the 9th century; explanations include soil erosion and deforestation, drought, warfare and loss of royal authority, with debate over their relative weight.')],
 ['wiki:Copán','wiki:Hieroglyphic_Stairway,_Copán',f'url:{U}129/|UNESCO: Maya Site of Copan|heritage'],img='Copán',dateNote='dynasty 426–c. 820 CE; stairway dedicated 755 CE'),
X('ciudad-blanca','La Ciudad Blanca (City of the Monkey God)',H,'disputed',2,1000,['oral tradition','archaeological','remote sensing'],
 "Legends of a 'White City' hidden in the rainforest of La Mosquitia, eastern Honduras, have circulated since the colonial era. In 1940 the adventurer Theodore Morde claimed to have found a 'Lost City of the Monkey God'; he never revealed its location. In 2012 an aerial lidar survey revealed extensive ruins in unexplored valleys, and a 2015 expedition, publicised by writer Douglas Preston and archaeologist Christopher Fisher, documented plazas, earthworks and a cache of stone sculptures from a pre-Columbian culture of about 1000–1500 CE.",
 [('Legend found','Media coverage presented the 2015 finds as the discovery of the legendary White City or a lost civilisation.'),
  ('Not one city, not lost','Many archaeologists objected that the region was known to contain numerous sites of a recognised Mosquitia culture, that no single \'White City\' has been identified, and that Indigenous groups were not credited; the legend and the archaeology are treated as separate.'),
  ('Morde\'s claims','Morde\'s notebooks reportedly show little sign of a real city, and some researchers suspect his account was invented or embellished.')],
 ['wiki:La_Ciudad_Blanca'],img='La_Ciudad_Blanca',dateNote='legend colonial era onward; ruins c. 1000–1500 CE; lidar 2012; expedition 2015'),
X('cadejo','El Cadejo: the spirit dogs of Central America',M,'myth-legend',3,1800,['oral tradition','mythological'],
 "In the folklore of Guatemala, El Salvador, Honduras, Nicaragua, Costa Rica and southern Mexico, the Cadejo is a supernatural dog that appears to people walking alone at night, often described with glowing red eyes and goat-like hooves. In many versions there are two: a white Cadejo that protects travellers and a black Cadejo that leads them to harm, especially drunkards and wrongdoers. The legend is widely told today and appears in art and literature.",
 [('Christian moral tale','Folklorists often read the paired white and black dogs as a colonial-era moral story of good and evil guardians.'),
  ('Older Mesoamerican roots','Some writers suggest links to pre-Hispanic beliefs about dogs as guides of the dead or animal spirit companions (nahuales); such connections are proposed rather than demonstrated.'),
  ('Sightings','People continue to report encounters, which researchers usually attribute to stray dogs, darkness and expectation; believers regard some experiences as genuine.')],
 ['wiki:Cadejo'],img='Cadejo',dateNote='recorded colonial-era and modern folklore; origins uncertain'),
]
# --- part 3: Arctic, Pacific Northwest, Philippines/Indonesia, Oceania
E+=[
X('inuksuk','Inuksuit: the stone messengers of the Arctic',H,'confirmed',3,1000,['archaeological','oral tradition'],
 "An inuksuk (plural inuksuit, 'that which acts in the capacity of a human') is a stone structure built by Inuit and other Arctic peoples across Alaska, Canada and Greenland. Inuksuit served many purposes: marking routes, travel directions and good fishing or hunting places, indicating food caches, and forming lines that helped steer caribou towards hunters. Figures built to resemble a person are distinguished by some Inuit as inunnguat. Enukso Point on Baffin Island, with more than 100 inuksuit, is a National Historic Site of Canada, and an inuksuk appears on the flag of Nunavut.",
 [('Practical landmarks','Most researchers and Inuit knowledge-holders stress their everyday functions as messages and tools in a landscape with few natural markers.'),
  ('Sacred stones','Some inuksuit are described in Inuit tradition as objects of veneration or associated with spiritual power, and should not be disturbed.'),
  ('How old?','Inuksuit are very hard to date; some may have been built by earlier Paleo-Inuit (Dorset) peoples, but most individual structures have no secure date.')],
 ['wiki:Inuksuk'],img='Inuksuk',dateNote='Inuit tradition from c. 1000 CE in the eastern Arctic, possibly older; most structures undated'),
X('sieidi','Sieidi: the sacred stones of the Sámi',M,'confirmed',3,1000,['archaeological','oral tradition','textual'],
 "A sieidi (seite) is a natural feature, often an unusually shaped boulder, rock outcrop or carved wooden post, regarded in traditional Sámi religion as a sacred place or being. Offerings such as reindeer antlers, bones, fish and metal objects were left at sieidis to secure luck in hunting, fishing and herding. Archaeologists have studied hundreds of such sites across northern Norway, Sweden, Finland and Russia, some with offerings dating back to the Iron Age and medieval period. During the 17th and 18th-century Christian missions many sieidis were desecrated along with Sámi drums.",
 [('Animist landscape','Scholars describe sieidis as part of a relational worldview in which features of the landscape are persons to be respected and bargained with.'),
  ('Living tradition','Some Sámi continue to treat sieidis with respect today, and several are protected as cultural heritage sites.'),
  ('Stone-age roots','Some researchers propose that the tradition continues very ancient northern rock cults; dating individual sites before the Iron Age is difficult and such claims remain hypotheses.')],
 ['wiki:Sieidi','wiki:Ancient_Sámi_religion'],img=None,dateNote='offerings from c. Iron Age/medieval era; tradition continues'),
X('potlatch-ban','The potlatch ban (1885–1951)',H,'confirmed',2,1885,['textual','oral tradition'],
 "The potlatch is a gift-giving feast of the Indigenous peoples of the Pacific Northwest Coast, such as the Kwakwaka'wakw, Haida, Tlingit, Tsimshian and Coast Salish, used to mark births, marriages, deaths and the transfer of names and rights. Canada amended the Indian Act in 1884 to outlaw the potlatch from 1885, and the ban stayed until 1951. In 1921, after a large potlatch held by Dan Cranmer at Village Island, dozens of people were prosecuted and masks and regalia were confiscated; part of the collection was later returned and is displayed at the U'mista Cultural Centre, opened in 1980.",
 [('Colonial view','Missionaries and officials claimed the potlatch was wasteful and an obstacle to assimilation and Christianisation.'),
  ('Economy and law','Anthropologists such as Franz Boas and Marcel Mauss presented the potlatch as a sophisticated system of exchange, status and law; Indigenous communities describe it as their governance system.'),
  ('Resistance','Many communities continued potlatches in secret during the ban, and the confiscated objects became central to modern repatriation movements.')],
 ['wiki:Potlatch','wiki:U\'mista_Cultural_Centre'],img='Potlatch',end=1951),
X('sgang-gwaay','SGang Gwaay: the village of standing poles',H,'confirmed',3,1860,['archaeological','oral tradition'],
 "SGang Gwaay Llanagaay (Ninstints) is a Haida village site on Anthony Island at the southern end of Haida Gwaii, British Columbia. It preserves the remains of longhouses and a rare group of standing carved mortuary and memorial poles. The village was abandoned in the 1880s after epidemics, notably the smallpox epidemic that began in 1862, devastated the Haida. Some poles were removed to museums in 1957; the site was inscribed by UNESCO in 1981 and is cared for by Haida Watchmen.",
 [('Let the poles return to the earth','Haida tradition holds that poles should be allowed to decay naturally; conservation is limited to stabilisation in line with Haida wishes.'),
  ('Museum rescue','Mid-20th-century museum teams argued that removing poles would preserve the art; the removals are now part of debates about consent and repatriation.'),
  ('Epidemic history','Historians debate how far the 1862 smallpox outbreak was worsened by colonial authorities expelling Indigenous people from Victoria; the scale of mortality is not disputed.')],
 ['wiki:SGang_Gwaay','wiki:1862_Pacific_Northwest_smallpox_epidemic',f'url:{U}157/|UNESCO: SGang Gwaay|heritage'],img='SGang_Gwaay',dateNote='village abandoned 1880s; UNESCO 1981'),
X('tabon-caves','Tabon Caves and the Manunggul Jar',H,'confirmed',2,-45000,['archaeological','radiometric'],
 "The Tabon Caves on Lipuun Point, Palawan, in the Philippines, were excavated from 1962 by Robert Fox of the National Museum. Human remains known as Tabon Man include a tibia fragment dated by uranium-series methods to about 47,000 years ago, among the oldest modern human fossils in the Philippines. The nearby Manunggul Cave yielded the Manunggul Jar, a burial jar of about 890–710 BCE whose lid shows two figures in a boat, interpreted as a voyage of souls to the afterlife. The jar is a National Treasure and appears on the Philippine 1000-peso note.",
 [('Early sea-crossers','The age of Tabon Man is used as evidence that modern humans reached the Philippine islands early, crossing water gaps.'),
  ('Dating uncertainty','Dates for the earliest layers have been revised several times; some fossils are younger than first thought, and the precise antiquity of occupation is still studied.'),
  ('Ship of the dead','The boat on the Manunggul lid is linked to widespread Austronesian beliefs about the soul travelling by boat, a connection most scholars accept though details are interpretive.')],
 ['wiki:Tabon_Caves','wiki:Manunggul_Jar','doi:10.1016/j.crpv.2004.06.004'],img=None,dateNote='Tabon Man tibia c. 47,000 years; Manunggul Jar c. 890–710 BCE'),
X('code-of-kalantiaw','Code of Kalantiaw',H,'debunked',2,1433,['textual'],
 "The Code of Kalantiaw is a supposed legal code issued in 1433 by Datu Kalantiaw, a chieftain on the island of Panay, with harsh penalties for offences. It became a fixture of Philippine textbooks and national memory, with monuments, an award and a historical marker. The text first appeared in 1913 in a manuscript, 'Las antiguas leyendas de la isla de Negros', presented by José E. Marco and attributed to a Spanish friar. In 1968 historian William Henry Scott showed in his doctoral thesis that the documents were fabrications, and later the National Historical Institute recognised Kalantiaw as not historical.",
 [('Forgery','Most historians accept Scott\'s conclusion that the code was invented in the early 20th century, likely by Marco, who also supplied other doubtful documents.'),
  ('Lingering belief','Some local traditions and older publications still treat Kalantiaw as real, and the debunking was resisted for years because the code had become a symbol of pre-colonial civilisation.'),
  ('What it hid','Scholars note the hoax overshadowed real pre-colonial evidence of Philippine law and literacy, such as the Laguna Copperplate Inscription.')],
 ['wiki:Code_of_Kalantiaw','wiki:William_Henry_Scott_(historian)'],img=None,dateNote='supposed date 1433; forgery published 1913; exposed 1968'),
X('java-man','Java Man and the Trinil engraved shell',H,'confirmed',2,-700000,['archaeological','paleoanthropological'],
 "In 1891–92 the Dutch surgeon Eugène Dubois, who had gone to the Dutch East Indies to search for a 'missing link', found a skullcap, a femur and teeth at Trinil on the Solo River in Java. He named the species Pithecanthropus erectus. The claim was hotly disputed for decades, but the fossils are now classified as Homo erectus. In 2014 a re-examination of freshwater mussel shells from Dubois' collection reported a deliberately engraved zigzag pattern on one shell, dated to about 430,000–540,000 years ago and attributed to Homo erectus (Joordens et al., Nature, 2015).",
 [('Missing link','Dubois presented the find as the link between apes and humans; later finds showed human evolution as a branching bush rather than a single chain.'),
  ('Earliest abstract mark?','The engraved shell is seen by its authors as the oldest known geometric engraving; some researchers are cautious about what the marks meant to their maker.'),
  ('Age of the fossils','The age of the Trinil fossils is usually given as roughly 0.5–1 million years, but dating of the Trinil layers remains debated.')],
 ['wiki:Java_Man','doi:10.1038/nature13962'],img='Java_Man',dateNote='fossils c. 0.5–1 million years old (debated); found 1891–92'),
X('degei','Degei, the serpent god of Fiji',M,'myth-legend',3,1000,['oral tradition','mythological'],
 "In Fijian tradition Degei is the great serpent god, a creator and ancestral deity said to live in a cave in the Nakauvadra mountains of northern Viti Levu. In one widely told story Degei found two eggs, kept them warm, and from them hatched the first man and woman. Fijian ancestral traditions also tell of the first chiefs, led by Lutunasobasoba, landing near Vuda. In some traditions the spirits of the dead go to Degei for judgement, and when he turns over in his cave the earth shakes.",
 [('Creation and order','Ethnographers read Degei as a creator and judge who explains the origin of people, crops and the moral order.'),
  ('Earthquake lore','The idea that Degei\'s movements cause tremors parallels other myths of a great creature beneath the earth, such as Japan\'s Namazu; whether these reflect memory of real events is speculative.'),
  ('Migration memory','Some writers link the Nakauvadra traditions to the arrival of Fiji\'s first settlers; how far such oral histories preserve real migration detail is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Degei','wiki:Fijian_mythology'],img=None,dateNote='traditional; recorded from the 19th century'),
X('marshall-stick-charts','Marshall Islands stick charts',H,'confirmed',2,1850,['ethnographic','oral tradition'],
 "Navigators of the Marshall Islands made charts from the midribs of coconut fronds tied together, with shells or coral marking islands. The curved and straight sticks represent ocean swell patterns and the places where swells bend and interfere around islands, which a navigator could feel through the motion of a canoe. There were different types, from teaching charts (mattang) to charts of particular island groups (meddo and rebbelib). The charts were memorised and generally not carried on voyages. Western observers documented them in the late 19th century, after colonial contact.",
 [('Unique ocean science','Scholars regard the charts as a sophisticated model of wave refraction developed without instruments or writing.'),
  ('Secret knowledge','Navigation knowledge was closely guarded by chiefly families, so much was lost; modern projects work with remaining knowledge-holders to revive it.'),
  ('How old?','No ancient charts survive, and the age of the practice is unknown; it is generally assumed to be much older than the first Western descriptions.')],
 ['wiki:Marshall_Islands_stick_chart'],img='Marshall_Islands_stick_chart',dateNote='documented from the late 19th century; tradition older'),
X('toraja-funerals','Toraja funerals and Ma\'nene',H,'confirmed',3,1600,['ethnographic','archaeological'],
 "Among the Toraja of the highlands of South Sulawesi, Indonesia, funerals (rambu solo') are the most important ceremonies of life. A dead person may be kept at home for months or years, treated as 'sick' rather than dead, while the family gathers resources for a funeral involving the sacrifice of water buffalo and pigs. The dead are placed in rock-cut tombs, cliff niches, caves or hanging graves, often watched over by wooden effigies called tau tau. In some areas a ritual called ma'nene is held every few years, in which remains are cleaned and re-dressed.",
 [('Cosmology','Anthropologists link the rites to the traditional religion Aluk To Dolo, in which buffalo help carry the soul to the afterlife, Puya.'),
  ('Tourism and change','Since the 1970s mass tourism has reshaped how funerals are staged and seen, and many Toraja are now Christian while maintaining the rites.'),
  ('Shared island pattern','Scholars compare Toraja practices with secondary-burial and ancestor-handling customs elsewhere in island Southeast Asia and Madagascar, such as famadihana, reflecting shared Austronesian roots; the depth of the link is debated.')],
 ['wiki:Torajan_people','wiki:Tana_Toraja_Regency'],img='Tana_Toraja_Regency',dateNote='tradition of uncertain age; documented since the colonial era'),
]
# --- part 4: 20th–21st c. mysteries, science, cryptids, hoaxes
E+=[
X('flannan-isles-1900','The Flannan Isles lighthouse keepers (1900)',H,'disputed',2,1900,['textual'],
 "On 26 December 1900 the relief vessel Hesperus reached the lighthouse on Eilean Mòr in the remote Flannan Isles, west of the Outer Hebrides, and found it deserted. The three keepers, James Ducat, Thomas Marshall and Donald MacArthur, had vanished; the light had not been seen since mid-December. Investigator Robert Muirhead of the Northern Lighthouse Board found storm damage at the west landing and concluded the men had probably been swept away by an exceptionally large wave while securing equipment. No bodies were found.",
 [('Freak wave','The official explanation, still favoured by most researchers, is that a large wave or surge struck the men at the west landing during or after a storm.'),
  ('Murder or madness','Some writers have suggested one keeper killed the others or that a dispute ended in tragedy; there is no direct evidence for this.'),
  ('Embellished legend','Wilfrid Wilson Gibson\'s 1912 poem \'Flannan Isle\' and later retellings added details such as an untouched meal and an overturned chair, and dramatic logbook entries that historians consider fictitious; stories of sea serpents, ghosts or abduction belong to this later legend.')],
 ['wiki:Flannan_Isles_Lighthouse','wiki:Flannan_Isles'],img='Flannan_Isles_Lighthouse',dateNote='keepers vanished c. 15 December 1900; discovered 26 December'),
X('mad-gasser-mattoon','The Mad Gasser of Mattoon (1944)',H,'disputed',3,1944,['textual','newspaper reports'],
 "In late August and September 1944, residents of Mattoon, Illinois, reported that a prowler was spraying a sweet-smelling gas into their homes at night, causing nausea, dizziness and temporary paralysis. Dozens of incidents were reported and local newspapers gave them sensational coverage. Police found no culprit and no trace of a gas, and officials concluded the episode was largely a case of mass hysteria; the reports stopped within a few weeks. A similar 'gasser' scare had occurred in Botetourt County, Virginia, in 1933–34.",
 [('Mass hysteria','Psychologist Donald M. Johnson\'s 1945 study argued that newspaper coverage spread anxiety and that symptoms followed the reports, a classic case study of collective behaviour.'),
  ('A real attacker','Some later researchers, including author Scott Maruna, have proposed a real perpetrator, such as a local man with chemistry training; these identifications are speculative.'),
  ('Industrial fumes','Others suggest that pollution from local chemical plants could have triggered some genuine symptoms, which fear then multiplied.')],
 ['wiki:Mad_Gasser_of_Mattoon','doi:10.1037/h0062339'],img=None,dateNote='31 August–mid-September 1944'),
X('ghost-rockets-1946','The ghost rockets of Scandinavia (1946)',H,'disputed',2,1946,['textual','military reports','eyewitness'],
 "In 1946 people in Sweden, Finland and other Nordic countries reported rocket- or missile-like objects in the sky; about 2,000 sightings were logged between May and December, peaking on 9 and 11 August. On 19 July 1946 witnesses saw an object fall into Lake Kölmjärv in northern Sweden; a military search of the lake found nothing. The Swedish defence staff investigated, fearing Soviet tests of captured German V-weapon technology. Their report concluded that most sightings were explainable as meteors, aircraft and other ordinary phenomena, while a minority remained unexplained.",
 [('Soviet missiles','Early official and press suspicion focused on Soviet tests launched from the former German base at Peenemünde; no evidence of such flights was found.'),
  ('Meteors and misperception','Investigators found that many reports coincided with meteor showers and that heightened attention after the war generated many misidentifications.'),
  ('UFO precursor','UFO researchers see the ghost rockets as a forerunner of the 1947 \'flying saucer\' wave and argue that some radar-tracked cases remain unexplained.')],
 ['wiki:Ghost_rockets'],img='Ghost_rockets',dateNote='mainly May–December 1946'),
X('higgs-boson-2012','Discovery of the Higgs boson (2012)',H,'confirmed',1,2012,['experimental'],
 "On 4 July 2012 the ATLAS and CMS experiments at CERN's Large Hadron Collider announced the discovery of a new particle with a mass of about 125 GeV, consistent with the Higgs boson predicted in 1964 by Peter Higgs, François Englert and Robert Brout, and by Gerald Guralnik, Carl Hagen and Tom Kibble. The boson is the visible sign of the Higgs field, which in the Standard Model gives elementary particles their mass. Englert and Higgs received the 2013 Nobel Prize in Physics.",
 [('Completing the Standard Model','Physicists see the discovery as confirming the last missing piece of the Standard Model, while leaving questions such as dark matter unanswered.'),
  ('"God particle"','The nickname comes from the title of Leon Lederman\'s 1993 book; most physicists, including Higgs, disliked it, and it carries no religious meaning.'),
  ('Doomsday fears','Before the LHC started, some critics claimed it could create dangerous black holes; safety reviews concluded there was no risk. Separate theoretical work on the possible metastability of the vacuum is speculative and concerns timescales far beyond the age of the universe.')],
 ['wiki:Higgs_boson','doi:10.1016/j.physletb.2012.08.020','doi:10.1016/j.physletb.2012.08.021','url:https://home.cern/science/physics/higgs-boson|CERN: The Higgs boson|institution'],img='Higgs_boson'),
X('h-pylori-ulcers','Helicobacter pylori: the doctor who drank the bacteria',H,'confirmed',2,1982,['experimental','clinical'],
 "For most of the 20th century peptic ulcers were blamed on stress, diet and excess stomach acid. In 1979 pathologist Robin Warren in Perth, Australia, observed curved bacteria in stomach biopsies, and with gastroenterologist Barry Marshall he cultured the organism, now called Helicobacter pylori, in 1982. Their 1984 Lancet letter linked it to gastritis and ulcers. To demonstrate the link Marshall drank a culture in 1984 and developed gastritis. Antibiotic treatment of ulcers became standard in the 1990s, and Marshall and Warren won the 2005 Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine.",
 [('Lone rebels','The story is often told as two outsiders defeating a sceptical medical establishment.'),
  ('Normal science at work','Some historians argue acceptance was fairly rapid by medical standards, with a US NIH consensus in 1994, and that scepticism was a reasonable demand for evidence.'),
  ('Forgotten forerunners','Spiral bacteria in the stomach had been reported by earlier researchers since the late 19th century, but their significance was not pursued.')],
 ['wiki:Helicobacter_pylori','wiki:Barry_Marshall','doi:10.1016/S0140-6736(84)91816-6','url:https://www.nobelprize.org/prizes/medicine/2005/summary/|Nobel Prize in Physiology or Medicine 2005|institution'],img='Barry_Marshall',dateNote='observed 1979; cultured 1982; self-experiment 1984; Nobel 2005'),
X('champ-lake-champlain','Champ, the Lake Champlain monster',M,'myth-legend',3,1883,['eyewitness','photographic'],
 "Champ is the name for a large, long-necked creature reported in Lake Champlain, between New York, Vermont and Quebec. Reports in local newspapers go back to the 19th century, and the best-known image is a photograph taken by Sandra Mansi in 1977 and published in 1981. The states of Vermont and New York passed resolutions in the 1980s protecting the creature, and Champ is a local mascot. It is often claimed that Samuel de Champlain himself saw the monster in 1609; his writings instead describe a large fish, the 'chaousarou', usually identified as a gar.",
 [('Misidentification','Sceptics attribute sightings to large fish such as sturgeon, swimming deer or moose, floating logs and unusual waves (seiches) in the long, deep lake.'),
  ('Unknown animal','Some cryptozoologists suggest a surviving plesiosaur or an unknown species; biologists note the lake formed after the last Ice Age and could not have harboured a plesiosaur lineage.'),
  ('The Mansi photo','Analyses of the 1977 photograph have proposed a log or other floating object; Mansi maintained she saw a living animal, and the location was never precisely identified.')],
 ['wiki:Champ_(folklore)'],img='Champ_(folklore)',dateNote='newspaper reports from the 19th century; Mansi photograph 1977'),
X('vinland-map','The Vinland Map',H,'debunked',2,1957,['scientific analysis','textual'],
 "The Vinland Map is a world map on parchment that surfaced in 1957, was bought and later donated to Yale University, and was announced in 1965 as a mid-15th-century map showing 'Vinlanda Insula', the Norse Vinland, west of Greenland, decades before Columbus. In 1973 chemist Walter McCrone's team reported anatase, a form of titanium dioxide first produced commercially in the 1920s, in the ink. In 2021 a full analysis by Yale conservators and scientists found titanium compounds throughout the lines and text, and an altered inscription on the back, and Yale declared the map a forgery. The parchment itself appears to be genuine 15th-century material, probably taken from blank leaves of a real medieval manuscript.",
 [('Modern forgery','The 2021 Yale study concluded \'there is no reasonable doubt\' that the map is a 20th-century fake; who made it is unknown.'),
  ('Earlier defenders','From the 1980s some researchers argued, partly from PIXE analyses, that titanium levels were too low to prove forgery; later work found errors in those measurements.'),
  ('Real Vinland regardless','The forgery does not affect the archaeological evidence for Norse presence in North America, confirmed at L\'Anse aux Meadows from 1960.')],
 ['wiki:Vinland_Map','url:https://news.yale.edu/2021/09/01/analysis-unlocks-secret-vinland-map-its-fake|Yale News: Analysis unlocks secret of the Vinland Map — it\'s a fake (2021)|institution'],img='Vinland_Map',dateNote='surfaced 1957; published 1965; declared forgery 2021'),
X('glozel','Glozel: the field of impossible tablets',H,'disputed',2,1924,['archaeological','radiometric','textual'],
 "In 1924 a young farmer, Émile Fradin, and his grandfather found a pit in a field at Glozel, near Vichy in central France. Over the following years thousands of objects emerged: fired clay tablets bearing alphabet-like signs, carved bones and stones, pottery and figurines. Physician Antonin Morlet promoted them as a Neolithic writing system far older than the Phoenician alphabet. An international commission of archaeologists declared the finds inauthentic in 1927, and the affair became one of France's great scientific scandals. Later thermoluminescence dates on some ceramics fell largely in the Iron Age to Roman era, while some bones gave medieval dates.",
 [('Hoax','Many archaeologists regard Glozel as a modern fabrication, perhaps with genuine older objects mixed in; the signs resemble no known script and the stratigraphy was poorly recorded.'),
  ('Genuine but misdated','Some researchers argue the site may be a genuine Iron Age, Gallo-Roman or medieval deposit, and that the tablets reflect a local use of writing; this remains unproven.'),
  ('Lost prehistoric script','Supporters in Morlet\'s tradition and some alternative writers still claim Glozel preserves a forgotten Neolithic alphabet; this is not supported by the dating evidence.')],
 ['wiki:Glozel'],img='Glozel',dateNote='finds from 1924; commission 1927; TL dates 1970s'),
X('chernobyl-1986','Chernobyl disaster (1986)',H,'confirmed',1,1986,['textual','scientific measurement'],
 "In the early hours of 26 April 1986, during a safety test, reactor No. 4 of the Chernobyl nuclear power plant in Soviet Ukraine suffered a power surge, explosions and a graphite fire that released large amounts of radioactive material over Europe. Two workers died on the night and 28 more emergency workers and staff died of acute radiation syndrome within months. The nearby city of Pripyat was evacuated on 27 April, and Sweden's detection of radiation at the Forsmark plant on 28 April helped force Soviet acknowledgement. An exclusion zone around the plant remains. Estimates of eventual cancer deaths vary widely, from about 4,000 among the most exposed groups (WHO-led Chernobyl Forum, 2005) to much higher figures in other studies.",
 [('Operator error','Early Soviet reports in 1986 placed the main blame on operators violating procedures.'),
  ('Design flaws','The 1992 INSAG-7 review gave greater weight to design features of the RBMK reactor, including its positive void coefficient and the design of the control rods, alongside a weak safety culture.'),
  ('Prophecy and fringe theories','Because \'chornobyl\' is a Ukrainian word for a kind of mugwort, some linked the disaster to the star \'Wormwood\' in the Book of Revelation; others proposed that a small earthquake triggered it. Both are speculative and rejected by mainstream investigations.')],
 ['wiki:Chernobyl_disaster','url:https://www.who.int/news-room/questions-and-answers/item/radiation-the-chernobyl-accident|WHO: Radiation: the Chernobyl accident|institution'],img='Chernobyl_disaster'),
X('spanish-flu-1918','The 1918 influenza pandemic ("Spanish flu")',H,'confirmed',1,1918,['textual','genetic','epidemiological'],
 "The influenza pandemic of 1918–1920, caused by an H1N1 influenza A virus, infected perhaps a third of the world's population and killed an estimated 17 to 50 million people, with some estimates as high as 100 million. It came in several waves, and unusually killed many healthy young adults. It was called 'Spanish' because wartime censorship elsewhere meant neutral Spain's press reported it openly. In 2005 scientists reconstructed the virus from preserved tissue samples, including from a victim buried in Alaskan permafrost.",
 [('Kansas origin','Historian John M. Barry and others have argued the pandemic began in Haskell County, Kansas, and spread through army camps such as Camp Funston in early 1918.'),
  ('European or Asian origin','Other researchers point to earlier outbreaks in the British army camp at Étaples in France (1916–17) or to northern China, with spread via the Chinese Labour Corps; the origin remains unresolved.'),
  ('Treatment as a factor','A minority hypothesis (Karen Starko, 2009) proposes that very high doses of aspirin contributed to some deaths; this is debated and not the mainstream explanation of the high mortality.')],
 ['wiki:Spanish_flu','doi:10.3201/eid1209.05-0979','doi:10.1126/science.1119392'],img='Spanish_flu',end=1920),
]
